 remain generation-agnostic monitoring records used in contemporary 5G anomaly detection [ 39 , 23 ] . No public labeled per-cell cellular DDoS CDR dataset exists; packet-/flow-level 5G corpora (5G-NIDD [ 40 ] , NCSRD-DS-5GDDoS [ 41 ] ) are not directly mappable to our 9 × 9 × 3 9	imes 9	imes 3 grid representation. Empirical claims are scoped to robust CDR-grid detection under realistic synthetic stress tests in a 5G-motivated CPS monitoring setting. VI-B Phase Progression Across Diff-DDoS Table IV summarizes detector performance across phases (all values at τ = 0.5 	au=0.5 ; Ph. 2–3 follow protocol P1 on the TabDDPM test split). Both architectures achieve near-perfect Phase 1 F1 ( 99.6 % 99.6\% – 100 % 100\% ) on multiplier-based test attacks—expected because Phase 1 multipliers (Table II ) deviate tens to hundreds of σ \sigma from normal (Fig. 2 ). Without retraining, Phase 2 F1 collapses to near zero for SMS, Internet, and Blended on TabDDPM attacks; ResNet50 silent-call F1 falls to 7.6 % 7.6\% despite greater model capacity (a comparatively deeper backbone). ADT (Phase 3) recovers ResNet50 F1 to 79.6 % 79.6\% (silent-call), 100 % 100\% (Internet), and 92.8 % 92.8\% (Blended) at round 3; SMS F1 stays below 10 % 10\% at τ = 0.5 	au=0.5 (a decision-rule artifact resolved in Sec. VI-G ). SimpleCNN silent-call F1 declines under ADT (Sec. VI-H ); for these sparse stealth scenarios (SMS flooding and silent-call), ResNet50 is the deployable backbone (Secs. VI-G – VI-H ). TABLE IV: Detector performance across phases (protocol P1; all values in %; deployment-default τ = 0.5 	au=0.5 ). Ph. 1: multiplier-based test attacks (Acc, Rec, F1); precision is 100 % 100\% for all rows (omitted). Ph. 2: TabDDPM-generated attacks without retraining. Ph. 3: ADT rounds R1–R3. SC: SimpleCNN; R50: ResNet50. Scenario Ph. 1 Ph. 2 Ph. 3 F1 Acc Rec F1 F1 R1 R2 R3 SMS (SC) 99.96 99.85 99.93 0 9.09 9.09 9.09 SMS (R50) a 100 100 100 0 6.98 9.74 9.58 Call (SC) b 99.85 99.40 99.70 52.63 38.44 12.8