res. These results confirm TabDDPM as a manifold-preserving stealth-attack synthesizer; limitations are discussed in Sec. VII . Fig. 2: Dual-axis realism summary on test attack grids ( 168 168 per scenario). Bars (left axis) show target-channel mean absolute z z -score, and lines (right axis) show joint (3D) MMD 2 to normal, for TabDDPM and multiplier attacks across scenarios. Lower values indicate closer alignment with normal CDR aggregates (higher realism/stealth). VI-D ADT Recovery and Multiplier Retention Beyond Table IV (TabDDPM test, protocol P1), we assess architecture-specific recovery and multiplier-test retention (protocol P5). SimpleCNN. On the same 335 335 -grid TabDDPM protocol (Table IV , τ = 0.5 	au=0.5 ), Internet ( 0 % → 96.6 % 0\%	o 96.6\% at R3) and Blended ( 0 % → 79.6 % 0\%	o 79.6\% ) improve markedly. Silent-call F1 drops from 52.6 % 52.6\% (Phase 2) to 9.7 % 9.7\% at R3—Grad-CAM analysis (Sec. VI-H ) shows ADT suppresses attack scores on TabDDPM silent-call samples (mean attack score on attacked cells 0.35 → 0.01 0.35	o 0.01 ) while preserving localization; ResNet50 Call F1 still rises to 79.6 % 79.6\% . SMS F1 remains near 9 % 9\% throughout. Cell-level localization. Per-cell accuracy heatmaps for the Blended scenario before and after ADT (Fig. 3 ) show that ADT converts a patchwork of inconsistent per-cell accuracies into a near-uniform 9 × 9 9	imes 9 accuracy grid ( ≈ 100 % \approx 100\% per cell), resolving spatial inconsistencies across attacked locations. TABLE V: Retention
